WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
What can political focus groups tell a
campaign that polling cannot?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.660
A poll can count responses to a defined
question.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.540
A group conversation can show how people
arrived at an answer and which

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.100
words they use along the way.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.920
Start with an issue people can describe in
their own terms.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.560
Then listen for the assumptions, questions

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.980
and concerns behind their first reactions.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.000
Introduce a draft idea only after you have
heard what they already

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.740
understand.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.220
For example, a civic team exploring a
public investment could hear that

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.880
participants support the goal but doubt
the timetable.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.400
That is a question for evidence and
clarification,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.560
not a promise that one phrase will change
votes.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.620
A focus group cannot predict an election.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.120
PRC Impact brings nonpartisan

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.440
qualitative and civic research experience
